  howItWorks: [
    {
      title: 'COFEPRIS licenses the facility, not the doctor',
      body: 'Mexico\'s federal health regulator, the Comision Federal para la Proteccion contra Riesgos Sanitarios (COFEPRIS), and its state counterparts (COEPRIS) regulate clinics, hospitals, pharmacies and surgical centers. A hospital or surgical facility needs a licencia sanitaria; lower-risk establishments file an aviso de funcionamiento at least 30 days before opening. COFEPRIS does not run a public search tool for facility licenses in the way the US runs hospital lookups; it publishes PDF lists of establishments found operating irregularly, and in April 2026 it launched a public viewer for medicine registrations. Ask the clinic for its licencia sanitaria number and the name of the responsable sanitario, and ask to see the document.',
      sourceIds: ['cofepris-irregular-list-2024', 'cofepris-medicines-viewer-2026'],
      link: { label: 'COFEPRIS on gob.mx', url: 'https://www.gob.mx/cofepris' },
    },
    {
      title: 'Consejo de Salubridad General certification is the Mexican hospital quality mark',
      body: 'The Consejo de Salubridad General (CSG) runs Mexico\'s voluntary hospital certification, now under the MOCEBPASS model, with manuals revised in 2025 and 2026. Its certification commission is required by its own rules to publish a prontuario of certified establishments and their validity dates. Certified facilities are a small and shrinking minority: the CSG\'s own list of establishments with a valid certificate, consolidated to September 1, 2026, has 15 entries nationwide and none in Baja California or Sonora, down from 86 on the May 2024 list, so a Tijuana hospital that markets itself as CSG-certified is usually citing an expired certificate. A 2019 study found under 1 percent of establishments certified in 2017. Several large private hospitals in Tijuana, Monterrey, Guadalajara and Mexico City also hold Joint Commission International accreditation, which ASMBS and CDC both treat as a reasonable minimum for surgery abroad. A small surgical clinic will usually hold neither.',
      sourceIds: ['csg-list', 'csg-list-2024', 'csg-rules-dof-2024', 'consultorsalud-csg-2025', 'asmbs-mt', 'cdc-medical-tourism'],
    },
    {
      title: 'Verify the doctor\'s cedula profesional and specialty cedula with SEP',
      body: 'Every licensed physician and dentist in Mexico holds a cedula profesional issued by the Secretaria de Educacion Publica. A general medical degree and a specialty (for example cirugia plastica or cirugia general) each have their own cedula. The public Registro Nacional de Profesionistas lets you search by name or cedula number, and since September 2025 issues a downloadable Constancia de Situacion Profesional with a QR code. Ask for the surgeon\'s full legal name and both cedula numbers before you pay a deposit, and confirm that the specialty cedula matches the operation you are buying. Dentists hold a cedula as cirujano dentista plus a specialty cedula (implantology, periodontics, prosthodontics, oral surgery); Mexico has no national dental board lookup, so the SEP registry and the clinic\'s COFEPRIS notice are the only public checks, and the ADA\'s consumer guidance says to arrange US follow-up before travel.',
      sourceIds: ['ada-mouthhealthy', 'expansion-cedula-2024', 'sep-cedula-portal'],
      link: { label: 'Registro Nacional de Profesionistas (SEP)', url: 'https://cedulaprofesional.sep.gob.mx/' },
    },
    {
      title: 'Board certification: CMCPER for plastic surgery, CMCOEM for bariatric',
      body: 'A cedula proves training; board certification proves an exam and periodic recertification. For plastic surgery the body is the Consejo Mexicano de Cirugia Plastica, Estetica y Reconstructiva (CMCPER), which publishes a directory of certified surgeons on its website and recertifies every five years. For bariatric surgery it is the Colegio Mexicano de Cirugia para la Obesidad y Enfermedades Metabolicas (CMCOEM), which lists certified surgeons online; most bariatric surgeons also hold Consejo Mexicano de Cirugia General certification. Many surgeons marketing cosmetic surgery to Americans hold only a general-surgery or general-practice cedula. If the name is not in the CMCPER directory, assume the surgeon is not a board-certified plastic surgeon.',
      sourceIds: ['cmcper-site', 'cmcoem-directory', 'advancestudy-cmcper-guide'],
      link: { label: 'CMCPER certified surgeon directory', url: 'https://cmcper.org/' },
    },
    {
      title: 'What US agencies have said: CDC, FDA and the State Department',
      body: 'CDC\'s medical tourism page lists wound and bloodstream infections, drug-resistant organisms, counterfeit medicines, language barriers and clots from flying as the main risks, and advises delaying air travel 10 to 14 days after major surgery. CDC and Mexican authorities investigated the 2023 Matamoros fungal meningitis outbreak at River Side Surgical Center and Clinica K-3, where 185 US residents were exposed through epidural anesthesia during cosmetic procedures. The State Department issued a counterfeit pill alert in March 2023 and keeps Tamaulipas at Level 4 Do Not Travel and Baja California at Level 3 Reconsider Travel in its State Department advisory issued May 29, 2026. FDA\'s regenerative medicine alert, updated April 2024, says no stem cell or exosome product is approved for orthopedic, neurological, autoimmune or anti-aging use.',
      sourceIds: ['cdc-medical-tourism', 'cid-matamoros-2024', 'statedept-pills-2023', 'rmn-advisory-2026', 'fda-regen-2024'],
    },
    {
      title: 'Facilitators and \'medical tourism companies\' are unregulated brokers',
      body: 'Most Americans reach Tijuana bariatric and cosmetic surgeons through facilitators and marketplaces that bundle surgery, hotel and shuttle into one price and take a commission. They are not licensed in Mexico or the US, do not carry malpractice liability, and their \'verified\' or \'certified\' badges are marketing terms, not regulatory status. Tijuana\'s own city government found that nearly half of the businesses buying medical fast-lane passes were pretending to be medical providers and removed them in 2025. Use a facilitator for logistics if you like, but verify the surgeon and facility yourself and pay the clinic directly where possible.',
      sourceIds: ['aol-fastlane-fake-providers', 'asmbs-mt'],
    },
    {
      title: 'Recourse: CONAMED, Mexican courts, and the limits of US law',
      body: 'A US malpractice suit against a Mexican doctor is rarely practical; US courts generally lack jurisdiction and Mexican judgments are small by US standards. Mexico\'s Comision Nacional de Arbitraje Medico (CONAMED) offers free conciliation and arbitration for complaints against public or private providers, with an online filing portal, a phone line (55 5420 7000 or 800 711 0658) and email. It can broker refunds or corrective treatment but does not award US-style damages, and ASMBS warns that compensation for complications abroad may be difficult or impossible to obtain. Some clinics sell complication insurance; read the policy, because many exclude the most common problems.',
      sourceIds: ['conamed-razon-2022', 'conamed-gob', 'asmbs-mt'],
      link: { label: 'CONAMED complaint portal', url: 'https://www.cavi-conamed.salud.gob.mx/vun/formatoweb.php?rd=007' },
    },
    {
      title: 'Paying with HSA or FSA funds and deducting the cost',
      body: 'IRS Publication 502 does not restrict deductible medical and dental expenses to US providers, so qualifying care in Mexico can count toward the medical expense deduction above 7.5 percent of adjusted gross income, and HSA administrators generally reimburse the same categories of expense. Cosmetic surgery that is not medically necessary is excluded everywhere, so a BBL or veneers do not qualify in Mexico any more than in the US. Keep itemized receipts in English or with a translation. Travel and lodging for care have their own limits in Publication 502.',
      sourceIds: ['irs-p502-2025', 'thomsonreuters-p502-hsa'],
    },
  ],
