  safety: {
    verdict: 'Mexico\'s safety record for American medical tourists has two distinct problems: clinical safety at the unregulated end of the market, and cartel violence in specific states. The 2023 Matamoros fungal meningitis outbreak and the same month\'s kidnapping of four Americans travelling for a tummy tuck both happened in Tamaulipas, a Level 4 Do Not Travel state, while Tijuana and Los Algodones in Baja California sit at Level 3. Documented harm clusters around small cosmetic and bariatric clinics with non-specialist surgeons, counterfeit pharmacy pills, and unproven stem cell and cancer clinics, not around certified hospitals.',
    points: [
      {
        title: 'Matamoros fungal meningitis outbreak, 2023',
        body: 'Between 1 January and 13 May 2023, US residents who had cosmetic procedures under epidural anesthesia at River Side Surgical Center and Clinica K-3 in Matamoros, Tamaulipas, developed Fusarium solani meningitis. CDC identified 185 potentially exposed US residents in 22 states; by 7 July 2023 there were 10 confirmed, 10 probable and 11 suspected cases with 8 deaths, and the CDC-reported death toll reached 11 by August 2023. A UTHealth Houston cohort published in the New England Journal of Medicine followed 13 patients of whom 9 died, with the fungus resistant to every approved antifungal tested. The source was never confirmed and both clinics were closed. A separate 2022 to 2023 outbreak in Durango exposed 1,801 patients and killed 40.',
        sourceIds: ['cid-matamoros-2024', 'krgv-toll-2023', 'newsweek-nejm', 'idweek-matamoros-2023'],
      },
      {
        title: 'Kidnapping and violence by state',
        body: 'On 3 March 2023 four Americans who drove from South Carolina to Matamoros for a tummy tuck were shot at and abducted by cartel gunmen; two were killed and a Mexican bystander also died. The State Department\'s State Department advisory issued May 29, 2026 keeps Tamaulipas (Matamoros, Nuevo Progreso, Reynosa, Nuevo Laredo), Sinaloa, Guerrero, Michoacan, Colima and Zacatecas at Level 4 Do Not Travel; Baja California (Tijuana, Los Algodones, Mexicali), Jalisco (Guadalajara), Chihuahua (Ciudad Juarez), Sonora (Nogales) and Guanajuato at Level 3 Reconsider Travel; Quintana Roo (Cancun), Nuevo Leon (Monterrey), Baja California Sur and Mexico City at Level 2; Yucatan at Level 1. Check the live advisory before booking, because levels change.',
        sourceIds: ['kidnap-matamoros-2023', 'rmn-advisory-2026', 'fox-l4-2026'],
      },
      {
        title: 'Cosmetic surgery deaths by non-plastic surgeons',
        body: 'Tijuana has had a string of US patient deaths after liposuction and BBL at clinics where the operator was not a certified plastic surgeon; in the January 2021 death of Keuana Weaver the FBI joined the investigation and reporting noted the doctor was not qualified as a plastic surgeon. Baja California\'s COEPRIS suspended 23 aesthetic clinics in 2022 and another 23 in 2023 for omissions and bad practice, and Tamaulipas suspended 13 in Reynosa and Matamoros. Fat embolism during BBL is a known cause of death in every country; the mitigation is a board-certified plastic surgeon in an accredited facility, which the CMCPER directory lets you check.',
        sourceIds: ['amspa-tijuana-fbi', 'zeta-coepris-2023', 'milenio-coepris-tamaulipas', 'cmcper-site'],
      },
      {
        title: 'Bariatric complications arriving at US hospitals',
        body: 'US border-region surgeons report a steady flow of leaks, abscesses and strictures in patients who had surgery in Tijuana days earlier, often without an operative report. The 2025 Surgical Endoscopy series and published emergency department case reports document ICU admissions and deaths. ASMBS says short post-operative stays abroad do not prevent complications after return, and that US surgeons may be reluctant to assume care. US insurers frequently deny coverage for complications of a non-approved elective surgery abroad.',
        sourceIds: ['surg-endosc-2025', 'ed-case-reports-bariatric', 'asmbs-mt'],
      },
      {
        title: 'Counterfeit pills in pharmacies',
        body: 'UCLA researchers who tested pills from 40 pharmacies in four northern Mexican cities in 2023 found counterfeits at 11 of them (27.5 percent): eight pills sold as oxycodone contained fentanyl, three contained heroin, and nine sold as Adderall contained methamphetamine, and the researchers could not tell counterfeits from genuine pills by appearance or location. Counterfeit sellers clustered in tourist areas with English-language signs. The State Department warned travellers in March 2023 and Mexico closed 23 resort pharmacies. Buy only from large chain pharmacies, never buy opioids, benzodiazepines or stimulants, and bring a US prescription.',
        sourceIds: ['ucla-pharmacies-2023', 'statedept-pills-2023', 'fox-pharmacies-closed-2023'],
      },
      {
        title: 'Stem cell and alternative cancer clinics',
        body: 'FDA states that no stem cell or exosome product is approved for orthopedic, neurological, autoimmune, heart, lung, vision or anti-aging use, and that marketed products may carry significant safety risks. COFEPRIS has said it has not authorized any clinical protocol for stem cell treatment of COVID-19, and Mexican stem cell clinics operate in a regulatory gap rather than under an approval. Tijuana\'s alternative cancer clinics have been documented in the oncology literature since 1991 as selling unproved and disproved therapies, and Baja authorities have closed clinics, including the unlicensed one where Coretta Scott King died in 2006.',
        sourceIds: ['fda-regen-2024', 'cofepris-stem-covid', 'pubmed-tijuana-cancer-1991', 'nbc-cancer-clinics-2006'],
      },
      {
        title: 'Travel clots after surgery',
        body: 'Surgery and long sitting both raise venous thromboembolism risk, and the risk stays elevated for four to six weeks after major surgery. CDC advises delaying air travel 10 to 14 days after major surgery; an NHS guideline suggests at least four weeks after a general anesthetic over 30 minutes; ASPS-quoted surgeons warn that sitting on fresh BBL grafts for hours on a plane can kill the transferred fat and that clots can form days or weeks after travel. A 2024 review specifically describes thrombotic complications in bariatric tourists.',
        sourceIds: ['cdc-medical-tourism', 'nhs-dvt-travel', 'asps-bbl-travel-2023', 'pmc-thrombotic-bariatric-2024'],
      },
      {
        title: 'The US system absorbs the cost of complications',
        body: 'The Montefiore series estimated the cost of treating medical tourism complications to the US system at about $1.3 billion a year, with the average complication costing about $18,000 and most paid by Medicaid. For the patient, that means a US hospital bill, or a denied claim, on top of the money saved abroad. Buy a complication insurance policy that names the procedure and covers US treatment, or hold the cash reserve to cover one.',
        sourceIds: ['asps-mt-2017', 'cdc-medical-tourism'],
      },
    ],
  },
